## Why this entry and not another
- **`template`** — `the service answers within {limit:number} milliseconds`
  matches the failing step's whole text with `200` in the `{limit:number}`
  slot (`{:number}` accepts digits, or the word `no`). The leading `and` of
  the Scenario's `and the service answers within 200 milliseconds` is the
  block conjunction, not step text, so it is not part of the template.
- **`block: then`** — the step sits in the Scenario's `then` block.
- **`means`** — the user's own words for the step, naming the template's
  `{limit}` slot, in product words a reader of E2E-001 understands.
- **`terms`** — `mcp service` and `reply` are both already in the
  `glossary:` of this document, spelled exactly that way. No glossary entry
  needs adding or changing.

## No-ambiguity check the applier should confirm after editing
- The new template's literal text (`... within ... milliseconds`) shares no
  shape with `the service answers "{reply}"`: that entry requires a quoted
  run between literal double quotes, which the failing step has none of, and
  the new entry requires the literal words `within` and `milliseconds`,
  which `the service answers "pong"` has none of. Each of E2E-001's two then
  steps therefore matches exactly one entry.
- `the mcp service is running` (given) and `the Operator sends "ping"`
  (when, via `the {actor:role} sends "{message}"` with the `Operator` role)
  keep matching their existing entries unchanged.
- No existing `template` value is edited, so no two entries share a
  template and no step another entry phrases becomes ambiguous.

